WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.990
Face it, tiger: Marvel Comics hit the jackpot
in 1966 when they introduced Mary Jane Watson

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:07.660
to the Spider-Man mythos.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:12.480
Over the years, she’s evolved into a complicated
and compelling character, and arguably the

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.590
greatest love of Spidey's life, and that long-lasting
prominence means that she's also been featured

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:20.250
in almost every Spider-Man adaptation.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:24.180
From Saturday morning cartoons to the big
screen, here's every version of Mary Jane

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:27.240
Watson ranked from worst to best!

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:28.980
Spider-Man ‘67

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:34.090
Mary Jane's appearance on the 1967 Spider-Man
cartoon, played by Peg Dixon, isn't really

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:35.090
bad.

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:38.180
Like everything that happened on this show,
it's just sort of inexplicable.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:43.880
While she has the name "Mary Jane," this version
is more of a mashup of MJ and Gwen Stacy,

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:46.510
and is even portrayed as Captain George Stacy's
niece.

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:50.510
Either way, it's pretty weird that one of
the major characters of the entire Spider-Man

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:55.940
saga makes her only appearance in the 52-episode
run in a story about how she gets duped into

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:01.579
go-go dancing at a club so that the Kingpin
can brainwash city officials with a mind control

00:01:01.579 --> 00:01:02.579
machine.

00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:04.780
Ah, the '60s.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:06.530
Spider-Man Unlimited

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:11.799
After Fox's popular Spider-Man animated series
ended in 1998, someone had the idea to reboot

00:01:11.799 --> 00:01:16.220
the show in a way that fans could choose to
view as a continuation, but that avoided everything

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:17.640
the show had already done.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.590
With that in mind, they launched a new series
with the truly wild premise of Spider-Man

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:27.400
following Venom, Carnage, and hero astronaut
John Jameson to an alternate version of Earth

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.880
populated mostly by human-animal hybrids.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:35.220
Oh, and instead of just his usual spider powers,
Spidey had a brand new nano-tech suit with

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:36.950
built-in robot weapons.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:39.330
Needless to say, it only lasted one season.

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:43.530
By necessity, that premise meant leaving most
of Spidey's established continuity behind,

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:48.619
so Mary Jane only appears briefly in the first
episode to give Peter the helpful advice that

00:01:48.619 --> 00:01:51.920
he should fake his death so he doesn't have
to be Spider-Man anymore.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:55.400
Jennifer Hale does a solid job with the few
moments she has, but there’s not much there

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:59.220
beyond telling Spider-Man that he shouldn’t
be Spider-Man anymore.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:00.810
Spectacular Spider-Man

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:04.950
The primary function of the Mary Jane we see
in the short-lived Spectacular Spider-Man

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:08.819
series, voiced by Vanessa Marshall, comes
from the bit about Peter Parker literally

00:02:08.819 --> 00:02:13.299
shuddering in horror every time Aunt May says
she has "a wonderful personality."

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:16.900
It's an update of the joke that accompanied
the character in her earliest comics appearances,

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:18.689
but here, it just lands flat.

00:02:18.689 --> 00:02:22.079
For one thing, Peter Parker actually seems
like a huge jerk.

00:02:22.079 --> 00:02:26.049
That in itself isn't a bad thing if he learns
a lesson, something that usually requires

00:02:26.049 --> 00:02:27.049
a dead uncle.

00:02:27.049 --> 00:02:29.439
This time, though, there's no real teachable
moment.

00:02:29.439 --> 00:02:33.379
Mary Jane’s arrival doesn’t really teach
him not to judge people by their appearances;

00:02:33.379 --> 00:02:37.849
it's more like he learns that people with
"wonderful personalities" can also be hot.

00:02:37.849 --> 00:02:42.059
Not quite as solid a moral lesson as the thing
about power and responsibility.

00:02:42.059 --> 00:02:44.069
Spider-Man: The New Animated Series

00:02:44.069 --> 00:02:48.760
Spider-Man: The New Animated Series was a
weird little experiment for a lot of reasons,

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:54.560
including a computer-animated style that definitely
stood out in 2003, but never quite worked

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:55.939
for web-slinging action.

00:02:55.939 --> 00:03:00.359
For another, the cast was stocked with celebrities
in an effort to hook the teen audience that

00:03:00.359 --> 00:03:02.589
was tuning in for MTV’s take on Spider-Man.

00:03:02.589 --> 00:03:06.879
That’s how we got Lisa Loeb, best known
for the hit single "Stay (I Missed You),"

00:03:06.879 --> 00:03:09.140
stepping into the role of Mary Jane Watson.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:12.919
She was a solid choice, bringing an entirely
different sort of character than we'd ever

00:03:12.919 --> 00:03:14.549
seen before with MJ.

00:03:14.549 --> 00:03:19.559
Unfortunately, the janky animation just underlines
that this Mary Jane that has zero chemistry

00:03:19.559 --> 00:03:23.370
with Spider-Man, although that probably has
less to do with MJ than with the cartoonish

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:27.560
Big Bad Wolf voice that Neil Patrick Harris
puts on to disguise his version of Spidey

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:29.019
from Peter Parker's friends.

00:03:29.019 --> 00:03:31.310
"Handy piece of information, that sword thing.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:32.310
How'd you know?”

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:33.310
"Long story.

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:36.209
But the point is, we make a good team."

00:03:36.209 --> 00:03:37.870
"Yes, we do."

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:38.939
The Spider-Man Trilogy

00:03:38.939 --> 00:03:43.950
Kirsten Dunst’s performance as Mary Jane
in Sam Raimi's cinematic Spider-Man trilogy

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:45.459
is a study in extremes.

00:03:45.459 --> 00:03:50.059
She’s the primary love interest of the entire
saga, although the third film flirts with

00:03:50.059 --> 00:03:51.849
throwing Gwen Stacy into the mix.

00:03:51.849 --> 00:03:55.479
Really, though, she’s just there as a prop
to make Mary Jane seethe with jealousy.

00:03:55.479 --> 00:03:57.290
And that's sort of the problem.

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:01.709
Throughout the films, Mary Jane's understandable
frustrations with Peter are presented to us

00:04:01.709 --> 00:04:05.589
as a callous misunderstanding of the burden
that he has to bear as Spider-Man.

00:04:05.589 --> 00:04:08.949
That's not entirely her fault as a character,
though; Peter deciding not to tell her his

00:04:08.949 --> 00:04:13.439
identity at the end of the first movie is
an almost inexplicable choice given their

00:04:13.439 --> 00:04:14.439
history.

00:04:14.439 --> 00:04:18.400
To make matters even more frustrating, she's
kidnapped by supervillains in all three movies,

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:22.490
and while that might be the default role of
a superhero's love interest, it gets repetitive.

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:27.490
With all that said, though, the Raimi trilogy's
MJ really does have a lot of great moments

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:32.080
too, including the upside-down kiss that stands
as one of the most iconic superhero movie

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:34.580
moments of the 2000s.

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:36.259
Supaidaman

00:04:36.259 --> 00:04:41.949
Toei's 1978 adaptation of Spider-Man as a
Japanese tokusatsu show didn't exactly line

00:04:41.949 --> 00:04:43.870
up with its source material.

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:48.240
Instead of a high school student being bitten
by a radioactive spider, for instance, dirt-bike

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:53.949
racing champion Takuya Yamashiro was granted
fantastic wall-crawling powers by a dying

00:04:53.949 --> 00:04:58.360
alien, which included a 50-foot sword-wielding
robot named Leopardon.

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:02.770
With that in mind, it shouldn't be surprising
that Takuya's love interest, Hitomi Sakuma,

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:05.289
is pretty different from her American counterpart.

00:05:05.289 --> 00:05:09.960
While Mary Jane was a model and actress by
the '70s, Hitomi, played by Rika Miura, is

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:12.120
actually a little closer to Peter Parker.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:16.370
She’s an intrepid photojournalist working
for a character that bears more than a passing

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:18.830
resemblance to J. Jonah Jameson.

00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:23.960
The only big differences are that Japan's
JJJ is actually Amazoness, the field commander

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:25.729
of the evil Iron Cross Army.

00:05:25.729 --> 00:05:29.920
Also, since Hitomi's actually taking pictures
of Spider-Man rather than just selling off

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:35.300
some extremely marketable selfies, it turns
out she's a better photojournalist than Peter,

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:35.980
too.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:36.980
Spider-Man ‘94

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:42.560
By the time Spider-Man hit the airwaves in
1994, only two years after the debut of Batman:

00:05:42.569 --> 00:05:46.860
The Animated Series and the X-Men cartoon,
the standard had been set pretty high.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:51.840
It might not hit the high points of BTAS or
have the memorable theme song of X-Men — instead

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:56.460
going with a truly bizarre techno song about
spider blood — but there's an entire generation

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:59.210
with some fond memories of this version of
Spidey.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:03.030
Much like the rest of the show, this Mary
Jane was a pretty accurate translation of

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:04.750
the character as seen in the comics.

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:09.080
She was a great foil for Peter Parker, and
Sara Ballantine would find her way into a

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:12.169
lot of Spidey readers' heads as the default
voice of the character.

00:06:12.169 --> 00:06:14.419
At the same time, she's not perfect.

00:06:14.419 --> 00:06:18.810
If nothing else, the idea that a fashion model
would be walking around in this outfit is

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:23.789
even less believable than a dude getting super-strength
from a radioactive spider-bite.

00:06:23.789 --> 00:06:25.960
Ultimate Spider-Man

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:30.919
With roles on The Powerpuff Girls, Teen Titans
Go!, and plenty of other animated projects,

00:06:30.919 --> 00:06:36.470
Tara Strong is one of the most prolific and
prominent voice actresses of the 21st century.

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:40.800
The fact that she spent four years playing
Mary Jane Watson and did a solid job of it

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:42.270
is hardly surprising.

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:46.180
Even beyond Strong's performance, Ultimate
Spider-Man‘s Mary Jane is one of the most

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:48.919
interesting and engaging animated takes on
the character.

00:06:48.919 --> 00:06:53.460
In a lot of ways, she’s mashed up with Lois
Lane: an aspiring reporter who's eager to

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:55.060
land a job at the Daily Bugle.

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:59.419
That gives her interactions with Peter (and
Spider-Man) an entirely different feeling,

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:04.240
and shifting the day job onto MJ allowed this
version of Spider-Man to be recruited by S.H.I.E.L.D.,

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:09.360
lead a team of teenage heroes, and have cross-dimensional
team-ups with heroes from throughout the Spider-Verse.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:15.020
Ultimate Spider-Man is a real hidden gem,
and MJ is a huge part of what makes it work.

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:16.569
Spider-Man: Homecoming

00:07:16.569 --> 00:07:21.999
If you want to get technical about it, Zendaya
doesn't actually play "Mary Jane" in 2017's

00:07:21.999 --> 00:07:22.999
Spider-Man: Homecoming.

00:07:22.999 --> 00:07:26.819
Instead, she plays a character named Michelle,
whose last name, Jones, and the fact that

00:07:26.819 --> 00:07:31.550
she goes by "MJ" around friends were kept
from moviegoers until the movie's final moments.

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:35.849
The reason it works is that Michelle’s personality
and characterization are completely different

00:07:35.849 --> 00:07:38.639
from what we've come to expect from Mary Jane
Watson.

00:07:38.639 --> 00:07:43.389
She has a bored, sardonic attitude replacing
the smiling popularity that we'd all expect,

00:07:43.389 --> 00:07:45.699
but that’s also what makes her the perfect
MJ.

00:07:45.699 --> 00:07:47.410
"I can't believe you guys are at this lame
party."

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:50.810
"You're here, too."

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:53.090
“...am I?”

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:55.569
Mary Jane has always been about subverting
expectations.

00:07:55.569 --> 00:07:59.650
Her first appearance in the comic was built
on the idea that Peter had been intentionally

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:04.020
ducking out on meeting her, and after Gwen
Stacy's death, she was finally revealed to

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:07.469
have a personality that ran a lot deeper than
how she'd been portrayed.

00:08:07.469 --> 00:08:11.900
Most importantly, the reveal that she'd known
Peter's secret identity since before she actually

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:16.620
met him redefined the core of her character,
and it’s hinted at in the way Zendaya's

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:19.159
MJ looks at Peter at the end of the movie.

00:08:19.159 --> 00:08:22.759
Homecoming's version might be called Michelle
Jones, but it turns out that a Mary Jane by

00:08:22.759 --> 00:08:25.440
any other name can be every bit as good as
the others.

